WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.880
[Music]

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:14.920
[Narrator:] The American Medical Association presents a special report in compatible color.

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:19.840
Ladies and gentlemen, the vice president of the United States, Richard M. Nixon.

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:26.270
[Vice President Nixon:] How do you do? In a few moments you will see a March of Medicine Program entitled MD International.

00:00:26.290 --> 00:00:33.430
I'm pleased with this opportunity to introduce it because I believe wholeheartedly in what it has to say.

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:37.870
President Eisenhower in his State of the Union message on January 9 stated,

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:44.760
"The only answer to a regime that wages total cold war is to wage total peace."

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:49.570
In this day of great concern with weapons in our search for security and peace,

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:54.160
this program documents a power some of us often overlook.

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:59.520
It is the force for good that lies in the basic brotherhood of man.

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:05.140
In my travels around the world I've often observed how a hand outstretched in friendship,

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:09.640
a heart full of goodwill, can do more to win the affection and support of people

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:12.750
than all the guns in our arsenal.

00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:15.800
The program you're about to see demonstrates this.

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:21.140
It is the story of American doctors fighting disease in distant, remote regions of the world,

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:24.210
helping fellow human beings to a better life.

00:01:24.230 --> 00:01:25.430
Through their humane work,

00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:29.920
they are making a positive contribution to the structure of peace.

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:34.630
Of course there are other ways to strengthen the bonds of friendship throughout the world.

00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:39.430
By helping other nations to improve their agriculture, their commerce, and their industry,

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:46.440
for example, our people can create a beneficial climate for greater international understanding.

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:52.040
And this is so essential to all our hopes for world peace.

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:55.780
Because our doctors abroad so bolster these hopes,

00:01:55.800 --> 00:02:01.690
we can regard their work as that of unofficial American ambassadors.

00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:05.980
And at a time when we hear much of man's inhumanity to man,

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:13.520
they reveal our greatest hope for the future, man's humanity to man.

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:21.910
That is why what you are about to see is so important to all of us.

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:27.510
[Narrator:] These are the changing patterns of the face of mankind.

00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:32.660
From the lips of such as these, in a hundred strange languages

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:40.040
comes a distant, unending cry for surcease from pain, and preservation of life.

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:47.310
Today we mark some who answer that call, American doctors on alien shores,

00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:50.610
whose dedication to humanity lights up our hope

00:02:50.630 --> 00:02:59.280
for peace and understanding in the far corners.

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:06.340
[Sounds of heartbeat]

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:54.340
[Music and background heartbeat]

00:03:54.350 --> 00:03:57.600
[Narrator:] The map of the world is more than seas and continents.

00:03:57.620 --> 00:04:00.480
It is as well the outline of its peoples,

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:03.590
each perhaps drawn a bit differently and colored by the creator

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:09.610
with a different crayon but all kin, one the member of the other.

00:04:09.630 --> 00:04:17.800
[Music]

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:21.930
This is the outline of the people of Pusan, Korea.

00:04:21.950 --> 00:04:24.810
So airplane-small is the world today

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:26.970
that whatever happens to these people

00:04:26.990 --> 00:04:32.640
defies boundaries and barriers to affect people everywhere.

00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:36.890
Street market or supermarket, such differences mean far less

00:04:36.910 --> 00:04:39.250
than our basic similarities,

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:42.890
and what alone holds the promise of healing our divided world

00:04:42.910 --> 00:04:45.910
is our concern one for the other.

00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:51.240
The story we bring you is of a special breed of men fired with just that concern,

00:04:51.260 --> 00:04:53.540
love of their fellow man.

00:04:53.560 --> 00:04:57.220
It is the tale of American physicians who have put on seven-league boots

00:04:57.240 --> 00:05:00.370
to giant-stride across water and land,

00:05:00.390 --> 00:05:03.730
and pause at the bedside of the world,

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:08.670
to lift the burden of disease from the backs of the family of man.

00:05:08.690 --> 00:05:10.920
We will meet them not only in Korea,

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:16.560
but in Hong Kong, Burma, Sarawak, India, Nepal, Lebanon, Ethiopia.

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:24.150
[Music]

00:05:24.170 --> 00:05:30.160
But first, first here in this alley in Pusan.

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:36.640
She whose back is tallest is clothed in the robes of the Maryknoll Sisters of St. Dominic.

00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:41.120
She and her sisters who live here are devoted to the sick, the halt,

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:50.010
the lame, the blind, and to this their clinic come the troubled ones.

00:05:50.030 --> 00:05:54.580
Look upon their faces and read for yourself their story.

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:57.290
The poor from the huts and shanties.

00:05:57.310 --> 00:05:59.540
The homeless from the streets,

00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:06.000
refugees swept south by the communist tyranny in North Korea, and the life-tossed.

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:09.740
They await only the opening of the gate.

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:19.250
[Music]

00:06:19.270 --> 00:06:26.430
And now to reap the day's harvest of illness nurtured in the overcrowded city below.

00:06:26.450 --> 00:06:29.170
Former patients under continuing care present

00:06:29.190 --> 00:06:33.610
the token of their previous visit and pass inside.

00:06:33.630 --> 00:06:44.900
[Babies crying]

00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:48.280
Then along the line to extract the seriously ill,

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:51.660
that they may be cared for at once.

00:06:51.680 --> 00:07:02.050
[Nun speaks to woman, who replies in Korean]

00:07:02.070 --> 00:07:21.990
[Babies crying]

00:07:22.010 --> 00:07:45.230
[Nun continues to speak to people standing in line]

00:07:45.250 --> 00:07:49.810
[Narrator:] A bond of pain have all in common,

00:07:49.830 --> 00:07:56.860
the wrinkled grandmother, and the wide-eyed innocent child,

00:07:56.880 --> 00:08:01.410
pain that oft times strikes them down as they wait.

00:08:01.430 --> 00:08:04.150
Thus is an act of the prelude to another day ahead

00:08:04.170 --> 00:08:08.980
as the nurse reports to the superior, Sister Angelica.

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:12.290
[Sister Angelica:] We have an awful lot of patients out there, we won't be finished early I'm sure

00:08:12.310 --> 00:08:15.520
[Nurse in white robes:] I have one little boy here I have to bring in though.

00:08:15.540 --> 00:08:19.560
[Sister Angelica:] Take the worst ones first.

00:08:19.580 --> 00:08:24.140
We'll seen more than a thousand patients a day coming through these gates

00:08:24.160 --> 00:08:27.140
before we close them at 5:30.

00:08:27.160 --> 00:08:30.680
People with every imaginable disease.

00:08:30.700 --> 00:08:34.170
We have no hospital, but we're building one now.

00:08:34.190 --> 00:08:38.680
We're just a clinic with no beds, so we treat them the best we can,

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:43.050
and send them home, to return if necessary.

00:08:43.070 --> 00:08:49.320
We have four sister-doctors and several Korean doctors and nurses on our staff.

00:08:49.340 --> 00:08:56.670
You will see them working inside to take care of these people.

00:08:56.690 --> 00:08:59.480
[Narrator:] Sister Gilmary, Women's Medical College of Philadelphia.

00:08:59.500 --> 00:09:04.050
Home, York, Pennsylvania.

00:09:04.070 --> 00:09:05.050
[A woman standing next to the child she has brought in for treatment speaks in Korean.]]

00:09:05.070 --> 00:09:08.670
[Sister Gilmary:] This little 14-year-old girl was carried on the back of her grandmother

00:09:08.690 --> 00:09:11.380
from the country to our clinic for treatment.

00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:15.590
She has a case of tuberculosis of the bone.

00:09:15.610 --> 00:09:19.600
We plan to treat her by putting her back into a plaster of Paris cast,

00:09:19.620 --> 00:09:23.420
which will prevent further crippling deformity of the bone.

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:26.930
It will also alleviate the severe pain which she now has,

00:09:26.950 --> 00:09:31.410
not only from the back but also from the abscess here.

00:09:31.430 --> 00:09:33.710
With the use of the new drugs for tuberculosis,

00:09:33.730 --> 00:09:37.380
as well as supplementary treatment with milk, food, and vitamins,

00:09:37.400 --> 00:09:40.580
we hope at the end of two years to have this little girl

00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:44.520
walking and playing like other children.

00:09:44.540 --> 00:09:52.590
[Narrator:] Sister Maria [?], Marquette Medical School. Home, Cincinnati.

00:09:52.610 --> 00:09:56.020
[Sister Maria:] This 53-year-old lady, Sun Yung Na,

00:09:56.040 --> 00:09:58.490
was brought to our clinic this morning.

00:09:58.510 --> 00:10:02.670
She has had in the past week severe symptoms of heart failure

00:10:02.690 --> 00:10:07.350
with a history of rheumatic heart disease since she was 30 years of age.

00:10:07.370 --> 00:10:11.540
She has had eight children and has gotten along well until now.

00:10:11.560 --> 00:10:16.540
She has severe symptoms of pitting edema of all extremities,

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:21.360
ascites, enlarged liver and heart with a murmur.

00:10:21.380 --> 00:10:23.900
We hope to be able to help this lady somewhat,

00:10:23.920 --> 00:10:28.430
but her prognosis in general is very poor.

00:10:28.450 --> 00:10:33.110
[Narrator:] Sister Lois, Marquette Medical School. Home, Hudson Falls, New York.

00:10:33.130 --> 00:10:34.680
[Sister Lois:] This is Kim [?].

00:10:34.700 --> 00:10:39.040
He was brought into our clinic approximately one month ago by his mother.

00:10:39.060 --> 00:10:43.150
The child's initial problem was one of infectious diarrhea.

00:10:43.170 --> 00:10:46.620
At the time of admission, he had a temperature of a hundred and four, six.

00:10:46.640 --> 00:10:51.680
We found, however, that the diarrhea and infection were a secondary problem.

00:10:51.700 --> 00:10:55.060
The child had been on his right side for approximately one month,

00:10:55.080 --> 00:10:59.600
and his malnutrition is his major problem.

00:10:59.620 --> 00:11:03.830
We'll continue to feed the child parenterally for a few weeks,

00:11:03.850 --> 00:11:05.660
until he's able to take food by mouth,

00:11:05.680 --> 00:11:09.380
and in a month or two he should look normal and healthy again.

00:11:09.400 --> 00:11:16.870
[Music]

00:11:16.890 --> 00:11:20.180
[Narrator:] As the day wears on and medications are dispensed,

00:11:20.200 --> 00:11:27.530
the city beyond as well beckons the Maryknoll Sisters with their healing arts.

00:11:27.550 --> 00:11:29.950
Each afternoon, Sister Gilmary,

00:11:29.970 --> 00:11:34.540
accompanied by a Korean nurse and immaculate in her white robes,

00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:38.330
plunges into the squalor of this suddenly expanded city

00:11:38.350 --> 00:11:43.540
on a home visit to a patient with tuberculosis,

00:11:43.560 --> 00:11:46.130
picking her way up an eroded hillside

00:11:46.150 --> 00:11:48.230
that is the front and back yard for hundreds

00:11:48.250 --> 00:11:53.750
who must live in tents or can boast only a oil tin roof over their heads.

00:11:53.770 --> 00:11:56.690
She struggles up the steep path herself,

00:11:56.710 --> 00:12:05.820
that her TB patients may be saved the exhausting journey to the clinic for treatment.

00:12:05.840 --> 00:12:34.120
[Rapid speaking in Korean]

00:12:34.140 --> 00:12:38.370
This one room is all the home there is for the entire family,

00:12:38.390 --> 00:12:42.220
living room, dining room, bedroom, kitchen.

00:12:42.240 --> 00:12:44.780
Not only is the mother sick with TB,

00:12:44.800 --> 00:12:47.180
but already father and child show traces

00:12:47.200 --> 00:12:53.110
of following her down the same threatening road, but for this help.

00:12:53.130 --> 00:13:42.000
[Nun speaks to family in Korean.]

00:13:42.020 --> 00:13:46.890
And now en route to another patient go the sister and her nurse.

00:13:46.910 --> 00:13:54.610
[Music]

00:13:54.630 --> 00:14:02.620
Thus is one day done, one day ended, as the gates to Pusan close behind them.

00:14:02.640 --> 00:14:15.120
America? America is 6,000 miles away and in their spirit and in their hearts.

00:14:15.140 --> 00:14:18.080
Moving south and west across the China seas,

00:14:18.100 --> 00:14:22.390
the March of Medicine halts in the shadow of red China to visit another American doctor.

00:14:22.410 --> 00:14:30.840
[Music]

00:14:30.860 --> 00:14:34.470
In Hong Kong, for centuries they said to one another,

00:14:34.490 --> 00:14:36.420
if leprosy can be cured,

00:14:36.440 --> 00:14:39.560
salted fish can live again.

00:14:39.580 --> 00:14:46.760
But here in 1946 appears a young pathologist from Chicago to teach at the University of Hong Kong Medical School.

00:14:46.780 --> 00:14:51.060
He rouses the community to fight leprosy, Hansen's Disease,

00:14:51.080 --> 00:14:54.600
until finally, not far from this communist island,

00:14:54.620 --> 00:14:59.090
the people of Hong Kong plucked hope from the sea.

00:14:59.110 --> 00:15:04.510
They call this island, Hei Ling Chau, the Isle of Happy Healing.

00:15:04.530 --> 00:15:08.300
And to this once barren place they move the long despised,

00:15:08.320 --> 00:15:11.820
disgraced and banished, to help them.

00:15:11.840 --> 00:15:15.310
And now a small launch comes bearing an interesting cargo:

00:15:15.330 --> 00:15:22.140
final-year medical students brought to break down their preconceived notions of leprosy, by Dr. Olaf Skinsnes,

00:15:22.160 --> 00:15:25.830
the man from Chicago whose inspiration the island was.

00:15:25.850 --> 00:15:31.310
He reminds them that the disease is not mysterious, punishment from the gods,

00:15:31.330 --> 00:15:42.340
not venereal, not inheritable, but scientifically explained and medically treatable.

00:15:42.360 --> 00:15:45.010
Here Dr. Skinsnes will leave them for a week,

00:15:45.030 --> 00:15:49.210
the island their classroom and international staff their teachers,

00:15:49.230 --> 00:15:54.690
before he returns again to review and summarize their findings.

00:15:54.710 --> 00:15:59.660
The next day the students are greeted by Superintendent Dr. Neil Frazer,

00:15:59.680 --> 00:16:03.090
who as China representative of the British Mission to Lepers,

00:16:03.110 --> 00:16:08.850
helped make the island a reality.

00:16:08.870 --> 00:16:11.270
No time is wasted in facing the young students

00:16:11.290 --> 00:16:14.150
with the different evidences of the disease,

00:16:14.170 --> 00:16:16.740
a disease which Dr. Frazer explains,

00:16:16.760 --> 00:16:18.960
as he points out some of its features,

00:16:18.980 --> 00:16:22.410
rarely if ever kills by itself,

00:16:22.430 --> 00:16:25.190
and of all the infectious diseases we know,

00:16:25.210 --> 00:16:28.570
one of the least catching.

00:16:28.590 --> 00:16:39.750
[Music]

00:16:39.770 --> 00:16:42.180
Choosing children housed on the island,

00:16:42.200 --> 00:16:46.850
he declares children the most susceptible to the ravages of Hansen's bacillus,

00:16:46.870 --> 00:16:53.070
which is often passed to them on contact in infancy.

00:16:53.090 --> 00:16:55.640
The students settle down to a week's labor,

00:16:55.660 --> 00:17:00.350
learning to overcome their fear of the disease by taking smears of patients

00:17:00.370 --> 00:17:04.200
to confirm the diagnosis of leprosy,

00:17:04.220 --> 00:17:08.460
examining the bacilli, breaking up under [?],

00:17:08.480 --> 00:17:14.310
studying bone changes responsible for much of the crippling,

00:17:14.330 --> 00:17:21.940
and finally they come not to be afraid of treating the patients themselves.

00:17:21.960 --> 00:17:25.630
Thus does each day bring a new understanding of the disease,

00:17:25.650 --> 00:17:30.180
as the students come ultimately, in sight and sound of boats in the harbor,

00:17:30.200 --> 00:17:35.520
to sit with Dr. Douglas Harmon of the island staff.

00:17:35.540 --> 00:17:38.920
[Dr. Harmon:] One thing that I want you to keep uppermost in your mind

00:17:38.940 --> 00:17:43.620
is that modern medicine can help these patients,

00:17:43.640 --> 00:17:46.330
and that with careful research,

00:17:46.350 --> 00:17:53.860
the progress that we have made in the treatment of their procedure in the last 15 to 20 years

00:17:53.880 --> 00:18:00.120
has made greater strides than in all the centuries before.

00:18:00.140 --> 00:18:05.760
This is a picture of a patient with lepromatous leprosy.

00:18:05.780 --> 00:18:08.140
Now I want to show you something.

00:18:08.160 --> 00:18:16.170
[Music]

00:18:16.190 --> 00:18:23.110
This is that same patient that you see in the picture there after a few years of treatment.

00:18:23.130 --> 00:18:27.440
And I think you will agree that with our modern drugs

00:18:27.460 --> 00:18:30.350
and with what we know about the disease now,

00:18:30.370 --> 00:18:34.690
that there is a real hope for recovery and for a useful life,

00:18:34.710 --> 00:18:38.500
and that this man is well on the way.

00:18:38.520 --> 00:18:42.020
I would like you to remember this man when someday,

00:18:42.040 --> 00:18:49.850
someone looking as he did before treatment comes into your office.

00:18:49.870 --> 00:18:54.510
[Narrator:] Later they watched the rehearsal of the lepers' production of a classical opera

00:18:54.530 --> 00:19:02.420
as they await the return of the doctor who brought them, their fears now dispelled by knowledge.

00:19:02.440 --> 00:19:07.190
So when at last the time comes to return to school with Dr. Skinsnes,

00:19:07.210 --> 00:19:11.830
they've come to recognize these sufferers as fellow human beings

00:19:11.850 --> 00:19:18.090
who can benefit from the same medical science and ideals they apply to other patients,

00:19:18.110 --> 00:19:22.610
thanks to the young doctor who came to Hong Kong from Chicago

00:19:22.630 --> 00:19:25.340
and his colleagues on Hei Ling Chau.

00:19:25.360 --> 00:19:33.610
[Music]

00:19:33.630 --> 00:19:35.570
Southward to the equator now,

00:19:35.590 --> 00:19:47.910
and the Malay Archipelago to Sarawak in northwest Borneo, sweeps MD International.

00:19:47.930 --> 00:19:50.070
These are former head hunters,

00:19:50.090 --> 00:19:56.280
the patients of still another American doctor who has carried his talents far from home.

00:19:56.300 --> 00:20:03.070
Iban tribesman, 213,000 of them living among the orangutans, the cobras,

00:20:03.090 --> 00:20:08.720
the leopards in the steaming equatorial jungle that is Sarawak.

00:20:08.740 --> 00:20:14.120
Here along the Rajang River in the land of the communal longhouse and the longboat,

00:20:14.140 --> 00:20:16.970
amid mangroves and sago palms,

00:20:16.990 --> 00:20:21.700
the forest has been cleared and a hospital raised where Dr. Harold Brewster,

00:20:21.720 --> 00:20:25.450
a Methodist missionary, assisted by Dr. Deng,

00:20:25.470 --> 00:20:29.290
has settled down and opened his black bag.

00:20:29.310 --> 00:20:32.930
[Dr. Brewster:] Our health problems here in Sarawak are threefold.

00:20:32.950 --> 00:20:36.010
The number one health problem is malaria.

00:20:36.030 --> 00:20:41.650
But the World Health Organization has a group here carrying on a campaign of DDT

00:20:41.670 --> 00:20:46.940
to eliminate this problem and in a few months we're going to see the effects of this.

00:20:46.960 --> 00:20:50.490
The second problem which we have here is tuberculosis.

00:20:50.510 --> 00:20:55.270
We're cooperating with the government by case-finding and treatment of cases

00:20:55.290 --> 00:21:02.830
that we find with streptomycin, PAS and IMH, which the government supplies us with.

00:21:02.850 --> 00:21:08.540
The third big health problem is intestinal parasites and the dysenteries.

00:21:08.560 --> 00:21:16.810
Of course this is tied in with lack of sanitation and ignorance of the rules of good health.

00:21:16.830 --> 00:21:21.470
We feel that it doesn't really solve anything to have these patients

00:21:21.490 --> 00:21:24.690
just come to the hospital and get treatment,

00:21:24.710 --> 00:21:29.940
and so we have a team which goes out, composed of a doctor,

00:21:29.960 --> 00:21:34.200
a public health nurse, an Iban helper, and a driver.

00:21:34.220 --> 00:21:38.020
We go out regularly in our longboat to the longhouses

00:21:38.040 --> 00:21:42.440
and live with the Ibans three or four days at a time.

00:21:42.460 --> 00:22:26.210
[Doctor speaks to patients one by one, in their native language]

00:22:26.230 --> 00:22:28.780
And besides treating the patients,

00:22:28.800 --> 00:22:33.750
we teach them the rules of health and hygiene

00:22:33.770 --> 00:22:37.120
which will enable them to live healthier lives.

00:22:37.140 --> 00:22:41.790
We do this by showing movies of health films and just sitting down with them

00:22:41.810 --> 00:22:43.790
in the evenings and talking to them,

00:22:43.810 --> 00:22:48.370
and explaining to them how they can be healthier.

00:22:48.390 --> 00:22:51.570
[Narrator:] Such is Dr. Brewster's medical work in Sarawak,

00:22:51.590 --> 00:22:58.730
where he struggles to bring the lifesaving promise of modern medicine.

00:22:58.750 --> 00:23:01.480
North and West travels MD International,

00:23:01.500 --> 00:23:06.480
skirting Thailand and the Gulf of Siam on its way to Rangoon, Burma.

00:23:06.500 --> 00:23:14.300
[Music and chanting]

00:23:14.320 --> 00:23:18.210
The air is filled with prayerful chants for rain.

00:23:18.230 --> 00:23:24.870
As the hot sun, the same sun that fires the golden pagodas, parches the land.

00:23:24.890 --> 00:23:29.380
From the Pazundaung, Kipling's waking, winking wonder of the East,

00:23:29.400 --> 00:23:34.660
the prayers to Buddha echo throughout the whole city,

00:23:34.680 --> 00:23:41.960
even to Rangoon's General Hospital, and in its wards.

00:23:41.980 --> 00:23:47.000
The patients know him as Dr. Green, orthopedic surgeon in charge.

00:23:47.020 --> 00:23:49.860
But they don't know how he went from Harvard to Turkey

00:23:49.880 --> 00:23:53.660
or the years spent with Yale in China Medical College.

00:23:53.680 --> 00:23:58.460
Today, Dr. Phillips Green is visiting professor at the University of Rangoon,

00:23:58.480 --> 00:24:03.000
often consulting with his Burmese colleagues such as Dean Minh Sang

00:24:03.020 --> 00:24:06.680
of the medical college whose patient, a Buddhist monk,

00:24:06.700 --> 00:24:10.710
is afflicted with tumor masses, disease nerve coverings,

00:24:10.730 --> 00:24:16.590
diagnosed as neurofibromatosis.

00:24:16.610 --> 00:24:19.920
And now Dr. Green comes to Yung Sanyoni

00:24:19.940 --> 00:24:23.290
who at 15 during the war was hit by an exploding bomb

00:24:23.310 --> 00:24:28.120
that killed his parents and took his left leg below the knee.

00:24:28.140 --> 00:24:29.820
If Sanyoni is to be helped,

00:24:29.840 --> 00:24:32.670
a piece of shrapnel still embedded in the lower pelvis,

00:24:32.690 --> 00:24:38.110
causing a mid-thigh chronic drainage must be removed.

00:24:38.130 --> 00:24:45.030
The doctor studies the x-rays in anticipation of the operation he plans.

00:24:45.050 --> 00:24:50.060
As he scrubs, his thoughts go back to the story Sanyoni told him

00:24:50.080 --> 00:24:52.530
of searching for help in vain,

00:24:52.550 --> 00:24:55.300
of his visit to a spirit woman in the hills,

00:24:55.320 --> 00:25:02.800
seeking a miracle that never came, of his despair of ever walking again.

00:25:02.820 --> 00:25:08.480
The plan is clear. First, to remove the shrapnel as is being done here.

00:25:08.500 --> 00:25:10.970
This to be followed by revision of the stump,

00:25:10.990 --> 00:25:15.010
repairing the painful bony spur at its end.

00:25:15.030 --> 00:25:21.460
This done, on to the next step, preparing for an artificial limb.

00:25:21.480 --> 00:25:27.130
Sanyoni watches, with hope mounting, the making of the mold of his stump,

00:25:27.150 --> 00:25:31.610
but actually far more than that, the overture, so to speak,

00:25:31.630 --> 00:25:38.240
for what most of us accept as a matter of course, life on two feet.

00:25:38.260 --> 00:25:44.100
And now, after the casting and the measuring and the straightening through physiotherapy,

00:25:44.120 --> 00:25:47.780
comes the most exciting day he has ever known.

00:25:47.800 --> 00:25:51.950
Where once was only one leg, now are two,

00:25:51.970 --> 00:25:54.460
one artificial and strap-controlled,

00:25:54.480 --> 00:25:58.190
to go with the one that is flesh and blood.

00:25:58.210 --> 00:26:04.880
And in step with the man who made it possible, Sanyoni walks.

00:26:04.900 --> 00:26:19.720
[Music]

00:26:19.740 --> 00:26:23.690
And at last in answer to the Buddhist chant,

00:26:23.710 --> 00:26:28.840
the long thirsty earth he moves upon drinks of rain.

00:26:28.860 --> 00:26:40.060
[Music]

00:26:40.080 --> 00:26:42.670
This time northwest soars the March of Medicine

00:26:42.690 --> 00:26:46.280
above the land mass that is India, to remote Nepal,

00:26:46.300 --> 00:26:51.710
at the roof of the world.

00:26:51.730 --> 00:26:54.860
To this Himalayan kingdom whose surrounding bastions

00:26:54.880 --> 00:26:58.580
have long served to seal it from the rest of the world.

00:26:58.600 --> 00:27:01.020
Yes, to this place too,

00:27:01.040 --> 00:27:05.190
have come American doctors, to Kathmandu,

00:27:05.210 --> 00:27:09.660
capital of the land of the gods, lying in a rice-sown valley,

00:27:09.680 --> 00:27:14.210
crossed by a sacred river, rich with a forest of shrines

00:27:14.230 --> 00:27:18.150
dedicated to Shiva the destroyer, and Buddha.

00:27:18.170 --> 00:27:28.410
[Music]

00:27:28.430 --> 00:27:33.060
[Dr. Fleming:] We came here from India in 1953.

00:27:33.080 --> 00:27:38.480
My husband had been here previously for the Chicago Natural History Museum.

00:27:38.500 --> 00:27:40.500
[Narrator:] You are listening to Dr. Bethel Fleming

00:27:40.520 --> 00:27:42.760
of Bucks County, Pennsylvania.

00:27:42.780 --> 00:27:45.010
[Dr. Fleming:] We became very attached to Nepal,

00:27:45.030 --> 00:27:47.500
learned to like the people very much,

00:27:47.520 --> 00:27:49.130
and were delighted when the government

00:27:49.150 --> 00:27:54.840
asked us to set up a medical program for maternal and child care.

00:27:54.860 --> 00:27:58.570
I arrived with only a little pocket set of instruments,

00:27:58.590 --> 00:28:02.840
just an obstetrical forceps and a stethoscope.

00:28:02.860 --> 00:28:06.680
Our first hospital was part of this old cholera hospital.

00:28:06.700 --> 00:28:10.650
That's all that was available.

00:28:10.670 --> 00:28:13.630
Then we moved here to Shanta Bawan,

00:28:13.650 --> 00:28:15.500
a former palace,

00:28:15.520 --> 00:28:20.610
where we have since grown to include two women's wards and a men's ward.

00:28:20.630 --> 00:28:22.230
[Narrator:] Wandering the hospital grounds,

00:28:22.250 --> 00:28:29.310
Dr. Fleming finds time for a couple of favorite patients.

00:28:29.330 --> 00:28:31.210
[Dr. Fleming:] This is Vishnumaya.

00:28:31.230 --> 00:28:37.550
She was discovered by the king while he was making a journey about eight days from here.

00:28:37.570 --> 00:28:42.730
Poor thing had club feet and was literally walking on her ankles.

00:28:42.750 --> 00:28:47.240
He sent her to us with a message to do what we could for her.

00:28:47.260 --> 00:28:50.890
Look at her now.

00:28:50.910 --> 00:28:53.610
This man is a Sherpa porter.

00:28:53.630 --> 00:28:57.500
He was a guide for a British climbing expedition.

00:28:57.520 --> 00:29:00.900
Three men were lost, buried by an avalanche.

00:29:00.920 --> 00:29:05.070
This fellow lost his footing. He fell 200 feet.

00:29:05.090 --> 00:29:33.370
[Dr. Fleming speaks Nepali to the patient.]

00:29:33.390 --> 00:29:37.040
[Narrator:] The eyes of this mountain man as he waits full of longing,

00:29:37.060 --> 00:29:43.170
and the eyes of the all seeing-consciousness are fixed on the Himalayas beyond Kathmandu.

00:29:43.190 --> 00:29:53.460
Snow-crowned, the highest of them, their names conjure a spell, Annapurna [?].

00:29:53.480 --> 00:29:59.170
Challenging their foothills and preceded by porters carrying supplies and camera equipment,

00:29:59.190 --> 00:30:02.140
the March of Medicine, in search of still another American doctor,

00:30:02.160 --> 00:30:04.940
perhaps more remote and inaccessible then any,

00:30:04.960 --> 00:30:11.140
move for two days on foot along this rocky, winding trail toward a town called Tansen.

00:30:11.160 --> 00:30:20.290
[Music]

00:30:20.310 --> 00:30:23.950
The paths cut into the mountainsides are known as well

00:30:23.970 --> 00:30:28.380
to Dr. Friedrichs [?] of Reading that he calls home,

00:30:28.400 --> 00:30:30.410
for time and again with only a pack on his back

00:30:30.430 --> 00:30:34.860
he must travel them as his highway to reach a patient in need.

00:30:34.880 --> 00:30:38.700
And often, return with him up the steep slopes to his hospital.

00:30:38.720 --> 00:30:48.940
[Music]

00:30:48.960 --> 00:30:53.680
This is Tansen, astride the trail to Tibet,

00:30:53.700 --> 00:30:59.570
and this is Dr. Friedrichs' hospital.

00:30:59.590 --> 00:31:04.180
In this isolated area, north to Tibet and who knows how far beyond,

00:31:04.200 --> 00:31:07.550
there is no other modern hospital.

00:31:07.570 --> 00:31:11.940
Later, the doctor diagnoses his litter case as kala-azar,

00:31:11.960 --> 00:31:18.710
black fever, a fly-borne disease often affecting the liver and spleen.

00:31:18.730 --> 00:31:22.750
In his primitively equipped ward he makes the rounds with his nurse,

00:31:22.770 --> 00:31:25.490
seeing first a TB patient,

00:31:25.510 --> 00:31:29.300
then encouragement to a little boy who, no different than any child anywhere,

00:31:29.320 --> 00:31:33.080
broke an arm falling out of a tree.

00:31:33.100 --> 00:31:36.460
And finally tending a man severely burned

00:31:36.480 --> 00:31:40.640
when he rushed into his flaming house to save what belongings he could.

00:31:40.660 --> 00:31:51.530
[Music]

00:31:51.550 --> 00:31:54.580
On the way to his semiannual checkup of the schoolchildren,

00:31:54.600 --> 00:31:58.180
Dr. Friedrichs passes through the marketplace of Tansen,

00:31:58.200 --> 00:32:01.590
streets incidentally that have never known the wheel;

00:32:01.610 --> 00:32:09.940
meets there a young lady whose broken wrist he once repaired.

00:32:09.960 --> 00:32:12.900
He continues on, greeted with a friendly shake of the head

00:32:12.920 --> 00:32:18.400
by those who have come to know the meaning of the word doctor through his efforts to help them.

00:32:18.420 --> 00:32:52.610
[Music]

00:32:52.630 --> 00:32:58.320
Gradually some kind of order is restored as the doctor tackles his first customer.

00:32:58.340 --> 00:33:01.370
Scabies, TB, malnutrition.

00:33:01.390 --> 00:33:03.810
These are diseases the doctor looks for,

00:33:03.830 --> 00:33:06.230
noting any evidence of them in a record book

00:33:06.250 --> 00:33:09.700
and following up later with his recommendations.

00:33:09.720 --> 00:33:13.500
As for the children, a doctor to feast their eyes upon,

00:33:13.520 --> 00:33:18.620
to thump their chest with his fingers and listen with his magic earpiece.

00:33:18.640 --> 00:33:24.070
These strange wonders come to their Himalayan hills are payment enough.

00:33:24.090 --> 00:33:36.290
And so from mouth to ears, with the headmaster looking on approvingly,

00:33:36.310 --> 00:33:38.760
the checkup happily continues.

00:33:38.780 --> 00:34:03.110
[Music]

00:34:03.130 --> 00:34:05.280
But finally with his assistant's help,

00:34:05.300 --> 00:34:07.110
the work is concluded,

00:34:07.130 --> 00:34:10.420
and turning over the records until next time,

00:34:10.440 --> 00:34:12.830
the doctor moves from one of the most pleasant aspects

00:34:12.850 --> 00:34:21.220
of his remote practice to confront a more serious challenge.

00:34:21.240 --> 00:34:29.110
From the distant countryside miles from Tansen comes one dread word, cholera.

00:34:29.130 --> 00:34:32.210
On the only means of rapid movement in this area

00:34:32.230 --> 00:34:34.660
go the doctor and his aide for the two-day journey

00:34:34.680 --> 00:34:39.570
out to a village in the path of the oncoming disease.

00:34:39.590 --> 00:34:42.280
They pause at the sight of the doctor's new hospital

00:34:42.300 --> 00:34:45.120
being built on land donated by the government,

00:34:45.140 --> 00:34:48.050
land no one else wanted because of the name,

00:34:48.070 --> 00:34:52.280
the Wedding Place of the Ghosts.

00:34:52.300 --> 00:34:53.650
They push on,

00:34:53.670 --> 00:35:01.640
passing through a settlement en route.

00:35:01.660 --> 00:35:04.930
And finally, after a night on the trail,

00:35:04.950 --> 00:35:07.750
they reach the village threatened by the epidemic

00:35:07.770 --> 00:35:11.020
and prepare to apply modern medicine as a barrier

00:35:11.040 --> 00:35:15.290
between it and the ancient disease.

00:35:15.310 --> 00:35:24.280
As the young and curious look on, the inoculations get underway.

00:35:24.300 --> 00:35:27.350
Each protective injection amusingly saluted.

00:35:27.370 --> 00:35:40.730
[Music]

00:35:40.750 --> 00:35:43.080
But as always when he visits a village,

00:35:43.100 --> 00:35:46.200
there's more than one thing to concern the doctor.

00:35:46.220 --> 00:35:53.150
Here, for example, a child gored by a bull.

00:35:53.170 --> 00:35:56.760
And so is one village made safe as medicine can help make it

00:35:56.780 --> 00:36:06.440
against the scourge of cholera, and the long trip home begins again.

00:36:06.460 --> 00:36:09.300
[Music]

00:36:09.320 --> 00:36:14.360
Hours later as night begins to fall and the winds promise a storm,

00:36:14.380 --> 00:36:18.770
they hasten to the protection of a rude shelter,

00:36:18.790 --> 00:36:21.840
and here will Dr. Friedrichs sleep.

00:36:21.860 --> 00:36:31.730
In the mountains of Nepal, as far from Reading, Pennsylvania as a man can be.

00:36:31.750 --> 00:36:36.870
And now, President of Smith, Kline and French Laboratories, Mr. Francis Boyer.

00:36:36.890 --> 00:36:39.310
[Francis Boyer:] How do you do?

00:36:39.330 --> 00:36:47.180
We're seeing a really inspiring story, a story being written by American doctors

00:36:47.200 --> 00:36:48.770
throughout the world,

00:36:48.790 --> 00:36:54.240
carrying their healing art to people less fortunate than ourselves.

00:36:54.260 --> 00:37:01.320
Perhaps too many of us take for granted the equally inspiring story here at home,

00:37:01.340 --> 00:37:08.660
the story of the doctors who are making our own lives longer, happier ones.

00:37:08.680 --> 00:37:13.910
Let me remind you of just a few things the medical profession has done for us,

00:37:13.930 --> 00:37:17.330
right here in the United States.

00:37:17.350 --> 00:37:20.770
This is one of the new heart-lung machines.

00:37:20.790 --> 00:37:25.220
During operations on the heart, it acts as a detour,

00:37:25.240 --> 00:37:29.670
actually carrying the blood around the heart.

00:37:29.690 --> 00:37:37.120
This machine gives the surgeon a relatively dry, bloodless area in which to work

00:37:37.140 --> 00:37:43.400
and enables him to perform delicate heart operations hitherto impossible.

00:37:43.420 --> 00:37:47.700
Here is a child crippled by polio.

00:37:47.720 --> 00:37:53.370
Where once it was commonplace to see little children struggling in braces such as these,

00:37:53.390 --> 00:37:57.090
soon it will be a rare sight.

00:37:57.110 --> 00:38:04.820
Today, thanks to the Salk vaccine, paralytic polio is on the ropes.

00:38:04.840 --> 00:38:08.910
Another example of what our doctors are doing for us:

00:38:08.930 --> 00:38:12.380
you may have read not too long ago,

00:38:12.400 --> 00:38:16.630
an article about the closing of the famous Trudeau Tuberculosis Sanitarium

00:38:16.650 --> 00:38:19.110
at Saranac Lake.

00:38:19.130 --> 00:38:23.600
Not only has the death rate from TB declined by more than 50 percent

00:38:23.620 --> 00:38:26.000
in the last 10 years,

00:38:26.020 --> 00:38:29.110
but the length of time spent by the average TB patient

00:38:29.130 --> 00:38:34.140
in a sanitarium has also been cut in half.

00:38:34.160 --> 00:38:39.110
Finally, let's take a trip to a modern psychiatric ward.

00:38:39.130 --> 00:38:44.530
Until 1956, each year there was an increase in the number of patients

00:38:44.550 --> 00:38:47.010
in our state mental hospitals.

00:38:47.030 --> 00:38:54.070
Last year, for the first time there was a reduction of almost 7,000 patients.

00:38:54.090 --> 00:38:58.360
This is the result of an unprecedented discharge rate.

00:38:58.380 --> 00:39:00.440
Today for the first time,

00:39:00.460 --> 00:39:04.710
there is new hope in the area of mental illness.

00:39:04.730 --> 00:39:09.320
Yes, ladies and gentlemen, your doctor at home, likes his colleagues abroad,

00:39:09.340 --> 00:39:13.490
is dedicated to the task of conquering disease.

00:39:13.510 --> 00:39:15.770
And because of this dedication,

00:39:15.790 --> 00:39:20.040
our nation is one of the healthiest in the world.

00:39:20.060 --> 00:39:23.330
May I add just one last thought?

00:39:23.350 --> 00:39:28.700
The mission of the pharmaceutical industry of which Smith, Kline and French is a part

00:39:28.720 --> 00:39:31.580
is service to the medical profession.

00:39:31.600 --> 00:39:35.530
I believe that our industry has done an outstanding job

00:39:35.550 --> 00:39:41.700
in developing and making available the drugs which help our doctors help us.

00:39:41.720 --> 00:39:45.920
The expenditures of our industry for medical research and development

00:39:45.940 --> 00:39:50.850
run well over a hundred million dollars annually.

00:39:50.870 --> 00:39:54.470
And of the medicine prescribed last year,

00:39:54.490 --> 00:40:01.750
more than half could not have been prescribed 10 years ago simply because they did not exist.

00:40:01.770 --> 00:40:05.920
Thank you.

00:40:05.940 --> 00:40:08.830
[Narrator:] Now across the breadth of Southwest Asia to Lebanon,

00:40:08.850 --> 00:40:12.480
crossroads of the middle east, sweeps the March of Medicine.

00:40:12.500 --> 00:40:17.700
[Music]

00:40:17.720 --> 00:40:20.180
Here on the Mediterranean sits Beirut,

00:40:20.200 --> 00:40:22.900
cultural capital of the Arab nations.

00:40:22.920 --> 00:40:25.100
And on the campus of its most famous institution,

00:40:25.100 --> 00:40:32.360
we are greeted by the Foreign Minister of Lebanon, Dr. Charles Malik.

00:40:32.390 --> 00:40:38.910
[Dr. Malik:] This all around me here is the American University of Beirut.

00:40:38.940 --> 00:40:41.840
I am a graduate to this university.

00:40:41.900 --> 00:40:46.770
It is an American University in more than name.

00:40:46.770 --> 00:40:58.830
It is American both in its democratic spirit and in its educational philosophy.

00:40:58.850 --> 00:41:06.360
It seeks to train its students to think freely,

00:41:06.380 --> 00:41:12.100
to value truth, and to live by principle.

00:41:12.120 --> 00:41:21.070
This is certainly true of its medical school, founded in 1867,

00:41:21.090 --> 00:41:26.780
before Pasteur and Lister had made their revolutionary discoveries.

00:41:26.800 --> 00:41:29.280
[Narrator:] The medical school that opened with 14 students

00:41:29.300 --> 00:41:33.290
now trains scores from all over the Middle East.

00:41:33.310 --> 00:41:36.580
The teaching staff, some of whom have trained in the United States,

00:41:36.600 --> 00:41:41.700
is drawn chiefly from Lebanon, but includes several American physicians.

00:41:41.720 --> 00:41:48.700
Heading the international faculty is an American, the Dean of the Medical School.

00:41:48.720 --> 00:41:52.740
American doctors teach pharmacology,

00:41:52.760 --> 00:41:55.930
anesthesiology,

00:41:55.950 --> 00:42:00.110
obstetrics and gynecology,

00:42:00.130 --> 00:42:05.190
surgery,

00:42:05.210 --> 00:42:07.120
and ophthalmology,

00:42:07.140 --> 00:42:12.130
all helping to train a student body that will spread the benefits of their medical education

00:42:12.150 --> 00:42:16.100
throughout this entire region of the world.

00:42:16.120 --> 00:42:19.360
Training goes on too in Lebanon's villages each summer,

00:42:19.380 --> 00:42:23.240
villages which often lack a suitable water supply.

00:42:23.260 --> 00:42:27.160
Here the students do fieldwork in preventive medicine in public health,

00:42:27.180 --> 00:42:31.020
under the direction of an American doctor who conducts these clinics

00:42:31.040 --> 00:42:34.470
that they may learn to help improve sanitary facilities

00:42:34.490 --> 00:42:41.340
and raise health standards.

00:42:41.360 --> 00:42:52.360
Now, typical of many students who are the product of this high quality of medical education

00:42:52.380 --> 00:43:02.670
is this young lady, Miss...or should I say Doctor, Nermeen Tutungi.

00:43:02.690 --> 00:43:05.980
Dr. Tutungi, where do you come from?

00:43:06.000 --> 00:43:07.450
[Dr. Tutungi:] Amman.

00:43:07.470 --> 00:43:09.560
[Dr. Malik:] You come from Amman in Jordan.

00:43:09.580 --> 00:43:12.190
Your brother was here too?

00:43:12.210 --> 00:43:14.380
[Dr. Tutungi:] I have two sisters and two brothers

00:43:14.400 --> 00:43:17.520
who have graduated from this university.

00:43:17.540 --> 00:43:18.930
[Dr. Malik:] What do they do now?

00:43:18.950 --> 00:43:24.330
[Dr. Tutungi:] My brother, one of them is with working in the obstetrics department here.

00:43:24.350 --> 00:43:26.300
My other brother went to the States,

00:43:26.320 --> 00:43:30.450
to Emory University in Georgia to specialize in cardiology.

00:43:30.470 --> 00:43:33.980
My sister is working [?] in Jordan.

00:43:34.000 --> 00:43:36.410
[Dr. Malik:] And what are your plans?

00:43:36.430 --> 00:43:40.210
[Dr. Tutungi:] I'm planning to go to the States next year to specialize in surgery.

00:43:40.230 --> 00:43:42.730
[Dr. Malik:] And then what do you expect to do?

00:43:42.750 --> 00:43:45.900
[Dr. Tutungi:] Our original plan was to go back to Jordan,

00:43:45.920 --> 00:43:49.430
each one with a specialty, and operate a hospital.

00:43:49.450 --> 00:43:53.750
[Dr. Malik:] Very good, Dr. Tutungi, thank you very much.

00:43:53.770 --> 00:44:01.530
Dr. Tutungi will, as she told us, carry back to her own land

00:44:01.550 --> 00:44:09.710
the skills, techniques, and designs that she has been able to learn here

00:44:09.730 --> 00:44:12.360
at the university,

00:44:12.380 --> 00:44:18.690
thus helping to build a better, a healthier,

00:44:18.710 --> 00:44:23.530
and a happier world for her own countrymen.

00:44:23.550 --> 00:44:26.160
[Music]

00:44:26.180 --> 00:44:30.260
[Narrator:] Next, south to the African continent goes MD International,

00:44:30.280 --> 00:44:36.380
over the Red Sea, Egypt and the Sudan, to Ethiopia.

00:44:36.400 --> 00:44:38.520
In the mountain city of Gondar,

00:44:38.540 --> 00:44:41.580
a few low-lying buildings give testament to cooperation

00:44:41.600 --> 00:44:45.570
for health among nations as well as individuals.

00:44:45.590 --> 00:44:48.980
Here our International Cooperation Administration,

00:44:49.000 --> 00:44:50.460
with the Ethiopian government,

00:44:50.480 --> 00:44:54.100
the World Health Organization, and UN Children's Fund,

00:44:54.120 --> 00:44:56.230
has established a hospital.

00:44:56.250 --> 00:44:59.130
Under the Director, Dr. Brooks Ryder of ICA,

00:44:59.150 --> 00:45:01.500
young Ethiopians are trained as health officers,

00:45:01.520 --> 00:45:06.040
community nurses, and sanitarians.

00:45:09.880 --> 00:45:11.600
At the thatched village of Kaladupa,

00:45:11.620 --> 00:45:16.420
an hour or more away, where the first of many planned health centers has taken root,

00:45:16.440 --> 00:45:18.860
the students who are the hope of Ethiopia's future

00:45:18.880 --> 00:45:22.760
practice preventive medicine by fighting the anopheles mosquito

00:45:22.780 --> 00:45:27.320
that carries the dread malaria.

00:45:27.340 --> 00:45:30.920
Here, among the descendants of the people of the Queen of Sheba,

00:45:30.940 --> 00:45:35.730
people of Hamidic origin speaking a language known as Amharic,

00:45:35.750 --> 00:45:40.490
American doctors seek to transfer their knowledge.

00:45:40.510 --> 00:45:45.640
Doctors like Arthur Curtis who heads ICA's Health Mission in Ethiopia,

00:45:45.660 --> 00:45:47.710
a long, long way from Arkansas

00:45:47.730 --> 00:45:53.480
and his oldtime job of directing a division of the state health department.

00:45:53.500 --> 00:45:57.240
Such health problems as are spawned by a primitive water supply,

00:45:57.260 --> 00:45:59.710
often secured from unclean streams,

00:45:59.730 --> 00:46:04.540
are now being assaulted by drilling wells deep in the ground.

00:46:04.560 --> 00:46:13.250
[Music]

00:46:13.270 --> 00:46:16.970
To the people, the bringing in of a new well represents

00:46:16.970 --> 00:46:20.560
a hard-won victory over the threat of disease.

00:46:20.590 --> 00:46:30.750
[Music]

00:46:30.770 --> 00:46:34.920
So that an elevated water tank, far from spelling an eyesore,

00:46:34.940 --> 00:46:37.940
seems a thing of beauty.

00:46:37.960 --> 00:46:41.250
Such is the progress born of international cooperation,

00:46:41.270 --> 00:46:44.400
symbolized by His Excellency, the minister of health,

00:46:44.420 --> 00:46:51.960
planning even greater projects with an American doctor.

00:46:51.980 --> 00:46:55.420
And nowhere is this more warmly felt than in the city

00:46:55.440 --> 00:46:58.530
and behind the royal gates of Addis Ababa.

00:46:58.550 --> 00:47:02.110
Here where his pet lion occasionally wanders the palace yard,

00:47:02.130 --> 00:47:04.570
one whose determined fight for freedom

00:47:04.590 --> 00:47:08.960
once moved the democratic world speaks his own feelings.

00:47:08.980 --> 00:47:13.170
His Imperial Majesty Haile Selassie the First, Emperor of Ethiopia,

00:47:13.190 --> 00:47:20.230
King of Kings, elect of God and conquering lion of Judah.

00:47:20.250 --> 00:47:22.740
[Emperor speaks in Amharic]

00:47:22.760 --> 00:47:27.250
[Narrator, translating:] We are grateful for the cooperation of your doctors.

00:47:27.270 --> 00:47:30.830
They have come at our invitation, far from home,

00:47:30.850 --> 00:47:37.160
in order to pass on their medical skills to our own people.

00:47:37.180 --> 00:47:43.590
Perhaps in no field does American cooperation have a more personal meaning for us

00:47:43.610 --> 00:47:50.210
since it directly touches the lives and well-being of our citizens.

00:47:50.230 --> 00:47:59.510
[Music]

00:47:59.530 --> 00:48:02.680
Finally across the wide span of the Arabian Sea

00:48:02.700 --> 00:48:10.180
to Vellore in South India marches MD International.

00:48:10.200 --> 00:48:13.030
This is Vellore's Christian Medical College,

00:48:13.050 --> 00:48:16.760
sponsored in part by more than 20 church groups in the United States,

00:48:16.780 --> 00:48:20.460
but actually the dream come true of one woman,

00:48:20.480 --> 00:48:24.080
granddaughter of the first American medical missionary to India,

00:48:24.100 --> 00:48:26.580
Dr. Ida B. Scudder.

00:48:26.600 --> 00:48:30.370
[Dr. Scudder:] I certainly am very, very happy to be here this afternoon

00:48:30.390 --> 00:48:35.890
and to welcome you young people as students in this medical college.

00:48:35.910 --> 00:48:38.580
When we started, you know,

00:48:38.600 --> 00:48:40.860
we had one student,

00:48:40.880 --> 00:48:44.790
and we've grown and grown and until we have so many.

00:48:44.810 --> 00:48:50.040
I'm getting rather old, but I'm very happy to be here with this group today,

00:48:50.060 --> 00:48:55.010
and I hope I should be here when you all graduate.

00:48:55.030 --> 00:48:58.030
And I hope you young people are going to go out

00:48:58.050 --> 00:49:08.240
and do great things for India and be representative of Vellore wherever you are.

00:49:08.260 --> 00:49:10.520
[Narrator:] To the villages surrounding Vellore,

00:49:10.540 --> 00:49:13.560
student doctors trained under an international staff

00:49:13.580 --> 00:49:16.350
move out in a mobile clinic to take the benefits

00:49:16.370 --> 00:49:18.610
of their clinic to their people.

00:49:18.630 --> 00:49:21.410
Founder Ida Scudder's niece, a doctor in her own right,

00:49:21.430 --> 00:49:24.210
accompanies this clinic on its journey,

00:49:24.230 --> 00:49:27.880
supervising the emergency work conducted at bus side

00:49:27.900 --> 00:49:30.930
and checking those patients who need her special attention.

00:49:30.950 --> 00:49:39.510
[Music]

00:49:39.530 --> 00:49:44.280
Besides carrying doctors to people, the mobile clinic carries its own pharmacy,

00:49:44.300 --> 00:49:47.230
dispensing modern medications to those long dependent

00:49:47.250 --> 00:49:50.910
on a few remedies found in nature.

00:49:50.930 --> 00:49:54.520
Many line up, too, to register their children for treatment for scabies,

00:49:54.540 --> 00:49:57.590
a skin infection caused by a small itch mite,

00:49:57.610 --> 00:50:03.000
often combatted in special campaigns of Vellore's rural health program.

00:50:03.020 --> 00:50:05.520
Once the way is cleared to the infectious areas,

00:50:05.540 --> 00:50:08.570
all bedlam breaks loose as the children are scrubbed

00:50:08.590 --> 00:50:13.110
with course coconut fibers to remove the diseased tissue.

00:50:13.130 --> 00:50:18.340
After they are treated, like newly washed laundry,

00:50:18.360 --> 00:50:21.180
they are put out in the sun to dry.

00:50:21.200 --> 00:50:26.560
[Music]

00:50:26.580 --> 00:50:33.230
In hands warped by leprosy, desperately clutching the air in exercise along the roadside,

00:50:33.250 --> 00:50:37.340
are the medical problems of India also reflected.

00:50:37.360 --> 00:50:41.340
Hope for such hands and those of India's million other lepers

00:50:41.360 --> 00:50:44.570
is found here in these hands at Vellore,

00:50:44.590 --> 00:50:49.350
undergoing special treatment by British orthopedic surgeon Paul Brand.

00:50:49.370 --> 00:50:54.710
[Dr. Brand:] These are leprosy patients, most of them uninfectious,

00:50:54.730 --> 00:50:57.330
although all of them have some hand deformity,

00:50:57.350 --> 00:51:00.390
and they're getting mobilized in preparation for surgery.

00:51:00.410 --> 00:51:03.150
Here's a lad you see, who's got typical claw-hand.

00:51:03.170 --> 00:51:10.010
He can't get the hand out straight because these four muscles are paralyzed.

00:51:10.030 --> 00:51:11.990
Now this boy had the same kind of hand, more or less,

00:51:12.010 --> 00:51:14.900
but he's had a reconstructive operation.

00:51:14.920 --> 00:51:16.710
You can see the scar back there.

00:51:16.730 --> 00:51:19.130
We took one of his good muscles in the forearm,

00:51:19.150 --> 00:51:21.760
and we took a tendon graft from his leg,

00:51:21.780 --> 00:51:24.890
and put the tendon graft right through his hand to each finger,

00:51:24.910 --> 00:51:27.260
and now you can see he's got...

00:51:27.280 --> 00:51:30.050
[Student:] But won't that muscle also get paralyzed?

00:51:30.070 --> 00:51:33.250
[Dr. Brand:] Well, that's a good point but no, it won't.

00:51:33.270 --> 00:51:35.870
We've surveyed several thousands of these hands now,

00:51:35.890 --> 00:51:36.860
and we find that in leprosy,

00:51:36.880 --> 00:51:39.940
there's an absolutely precise pattern of relatus.

00:51:39.960 --> 00:51:42.420
It always hits certain muscles and always spares certain muscles.

00:51:42.440 --> 00:51:44.630
[Student:] Is that a pattern you worked out here at Vellore?

00:51:44.650 --> 00:51:49.280
[Dr. Brand:] That's right. Now this boy's had an operation on his other hand three weeks ago,

00:51:49.300 --> 00:51:53.670
so in about a month's time he should be fit for doing good work.

00:51:53.690 --> 00:51:55.390
And that's really the important thing,

00:51:55.410 --> 00:51:58.710
you see, these men, you can cure them of leprosy now,

00:51:58.730 --> 00:52:00.650
but what's the use of being cured of leprosy

00:52:00.670 --> 00:52:03.620
if you've got a deformed hand and an ugly face and you can't get back to your family,

00:52:03.640 --> 00:52:05.730
you can't get back to your job?

00:52:05.750 --> 00:52:09.660
So by giving them new noses and new eyebrows and working fingers,

00:52:09.680 --> 00:52:19.220
they can take a job and they can go back to normal lives.

00:52:19.240 --> 00:52:22.640
[Narrator:] In the hospital in Vellore, a serious operation proceeds

00:52:22.660 --> 00:52:26.970
under a doctor from Boston.

00:52:26.990 --> 00:52:30.080
A four-year old child suffering from a heart defect

00:52:30.100 --> 00:52:31.220
with which he was born,

00:52:31.220 --> 00:52:36.420
undergoes what is known as a Blalock-Taussig, or blue baby, operation

00:52:36.450 --> 00:52:39.380
to restore more normal blood circulation,

00:52:39.400 --> 00:52:46.810
a type of surgery never performed at Vellore until the arrival of thoracic surgeon Dr. Reeve Betts.

00:52:46.830 --> 00:52:49.790
Assisted by Dr. Gopinat, soon to study in the States,

00:52:49.810 --> 00:52:52.070
and other members of the Indian staff,

00:52:52.090 --> 00:52:56.840
Dr. Betts introduces a new and needed blood supply to the exposed heart

00:52:56.860 --> 00:53:02.940
by bringing down an artery to the arm and joining it to an artery to the lung.

00:53:02.960 --> 00:53:05.410
The entire procedure is observed by student doctors

00:53:05.430 --> 00:53:14.130
in constant training under the new chest surgery teaching program.

00:53:14.150 --> 00:53:17.590
Dr. Betts checks the newly introduced blood flow

00:53:17.610 --> 00:53:24.260
by comparing the color of his fingernails with the fast returning color of the child's.

00:53:24.280 --> 00:53:30.410
[Voiceover:] And Jesus commanded him to be brought unto Him saying, what would thou that I should do unto thee?

00:53:30.430 --> 00:53:36.020
And he said Lord, that I may receive my sight.

00:53:36.040 --> 00:53:42.310
[Narrator:] In the same spirit Dr. Victor Rambo dedicates his life and medical talents that the blind may see.

00:53:42.330 --> 00:53:44.750
Raised in Nebraska and Wyoming,

00:53:44.770 --> 00:53:47.000
schooled in Kansas and Pennsylvania,

00:53:47.020 --> 00:53:50.730
Dr. Rambo initiated Vellore's eye camp,

00:53:50.750 --> 00:53:56.810
a means of carrying eye surgery out into the field by trailer to treat those who otherwise might never come

00:53:56.830 --> 00:54:01.370
to experience the healing power of medical science.

00:54:01.390 --> 00:54:09.310
[Music]

00:54:09.330 --> 00:54:12.570
Wherever a makeshift clinic can be set up for a few days,

00:54:12.590 --> 00:54:17.180
he brings relief to the many thousands in India blinded by cataracts,

00:54:17.200 --> 00:54:21.460
the clouding of the lens inside the eye.

00:54:24.240 --> 00:54:27.810
Entering the eye made insensitive by anesthesia,

00:54:27.830 --> 00:54:32.180
Dr. Rambo goes down behind the iris,

00:54:32.200 --> 00:54:36.320
grasps the clouded lens at the bottom with his forceps,

00:54:36.340 --> 00:54:47.180
rocks it free of its moorings and tumbles it up and out.

00:54:47.200 --> 00:54:54.220
Later on glasses will provide an artificial lens to substitute for the one removed.

00:54:54.240 --> 00:54:56.730
Few men are more dedicated to their task in life

00:54:56.750 --> 00:55:01.430
and more enthusiastic about it then Dr. Victor Rambo.

00:55:01.450 --> 00:55:04.600
[Dr. Rambo:] I've just taken the cataracts out of an old man

00:55:04.620 --> 00:55:07.270
who we wouldn't have reached if we hadn't come out,

00:55:07.290 --> 00:55:12.200
on the 350th eye camp, out to the villages of India

00:55:12.220 --> 00:55:15.220
where most of the people of India live,

00:55:15.240 --> 00:55:17.930
and where there are a million people blind

00:55:17.950 --> 00:55:20.720
whom our surgery doesn't reach.

00:55:20.740 --> 00:55:23.600
Now our surgery should reach all of these,

00:55:23.620 --> 00:55:27.930
so we are, for the love of God and the friendship of the American people,

00:55:27.950 --> 00:55:29.840
and the friendship of the Indian people,

00:55:29.860 --> 00:55:35.370
joining together to really try and reach the very fellow in the village

00:55:35.390 --> 00:55:40.360
and we are glad that are reaching him.

00:55:40.380 --> 00:55:41.600
[Narrator:] One by one,

00:55:41.620 --> 00:55:46.220
sometimes the number totaling as many as a hundred operated on in a single day,

00:55:46.240 --> 00:55:50.250
the patients, their eyes bandaged and protected from infection

00:55:50.270 --> 00:55:54.890
by drops of an antibiotic, are brought out and temporarily placed in the courtyard

00:55:54.910 --> 00:56:01.530
of the improvised hospital to rest...to rest and wait for the coming of the light,

00:56:01.550 --> 00:56:04.520
light that will remove the burden of darkness from them

00:56:04.540 --> 00:56:07.070
that so long has made them an object of pity,

00:56:07.090 --> 00:56:10.110
and save them from a lifetime of begging,

00:56:10.130 --> 00:56:13.800
the traditional occupation of the blind.

00:56:13.820 --> 00:56:18.780
This, to these, is the true meaning of the surgeon's mask,

00:56:18.800 --> 00:56:27.620
of the instruments that boil on the kerosene stove and the needle that gave them peace.

00:56:28.430 --> 00:56:34.430
A week or so later Dr. Rambo returns to the patients he left in care of a small staff,

00:56:34.450 --> 00:56:39.720
returns to remove the stitches from the operated eyes and check their progress.

00:56:39.740 --> 00:56:48.980
To conserve time he works over two patients at once.

00:56:49.000 --> 00:56:51.750
The bandages are permanently removed,

00:56:51.770 --> 00:56:57.700
but the shields are reapplied and worn for about two more days.

00:56:57.720 --> 00:57:07.420
[Birds singing]

00:57:07.440 --> 00:57:14.400
In one eye after the other he finds the promise of what is to come.

00:57:14.420 --> 00:57:21.810
Finally, the day to receive their glasses, glasses that will be their new windows on the world,

00:57:21.830 --> 00:57:28.090
a world with darkness vanished, hope rekindled, spirit reborn.

00:57:28.110 --> 00:57:40.730
[Music]

00:57:40.750 --> 00:57:47.650
This then is the time for parting, the time for thank you and goodbye

00:57:47.670 --> 00:57:55.010
in ways far more expressive than words to the tall spare doctor from America.

00:57:57.130 --> 00:58:03.540
As he stands there, in a remote village of India, surrounded by the sick he healed,

00:58:03.560 --> 00:58:09.610
see in your mind's eye the multiplication of him throughout the whole world,

00:58:09.630 --> 00:58:15.090
and discover not only the blind, homeward bound, seeing again,

00:58:15.110 --> 00:58:20.330
but all the earth's peoples seeing that there is hope for peace

00:58:20.350 --> 00:58:23.470
when man is his brother's keeper.

00:58:23.490 --> 00:58:37.780
[Music]

00:58:40.530 --> 00:58:42.790
The March of Medicine,

00:58:42.810 --> 00:58:44.380
presented by the American Medical Association

00:58:49.810 --> 00:58:52.800
in cooperation with Smith, Kline and French Laboratories.

00:58:56.190 --> 00:58:59.740
Produced for the Medical Television Unit, Smith, Kline and French Laboratories.

00:59:00.180 --> 00:59:27.180
[Music]

